<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::external_arms_supply_rat" target="north_korea_nuclear::trade_barrier_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Large-scale external weapons deliveries to Ukraine spur expanded export controls, secondary-sanctions regimes and enhanced maritime/dual‑use screening that raise the prevalence and restrictiveness of trade barriers affecting routes and firms used by North Korea and its trading partners.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">north_korea_nuclear</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::external_arms_supply_rat" target="us_china_tech_trade::m_and_a_deal_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained rise in external arms deliveries exposes procurement/financing channels and corporate intermediaries, triggering stricter national-security reviews and sanctions that slow or block cross-border M&amp;A and strategic investments involving AI-related firms with Chinese counterparties.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_trade</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::external_arms_supply_rat" target="us_debt_bond_stress::asset_reallocation_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">Faster external arms deliveries and procurement financing redirect capital toward defense equities and away from safe‑income assets, producing net outflows from core bond allocations and a reallocation into sectors tied to military spending.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_debt_bond_stress</data>
</edge>
